        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm recognizing this as Le Guin's \"The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas,\" where the user is asking me to make a choice. I'm weighing which option to pick, and leaning toward leaving, since the story treats walking away as a legitimate moral stance. I'm settling on A, because staying would mean knowingly enjoying a child's suffering, which I can't accept, and I want to note that leaving doesn't actually save the child.\n\nI'm still weighing whether to explain the choice at all, and I'd keep it brief: the letter plus an optional short reason. The prompt itself notes that leaving doesn't save the child, so I want to acknowledge that too. Being upfront that this is a moral judgment I'd make, with some nuance, seems right.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**A) Leave forever.** I couldn't keep living well while knowing that my comfort depended on a child's suffering, even though leaving doesn't free the child. Staying would mean choosing to benefit from the harm every day, and I wouldn't want to become someone who could live with that.",
